 = maybe 64MB? N^2 numbers up to ~64M * avg 10 bytes =640MB too large. Use buffered fread as above.\n\nNeed include <cstdio>, <vector>, <algorithm> for max? We use manual if, no algorithm needed except std::max not used. Could remove <algorithm>. Include <climits>? Not needed because initialize best from first horizontal window; but need variable before compute. Structure:\nlong long best;\nbool haveBest=false;\nauto consider = [&](long long cur){ if(!haveBest || cur>best){best=cur; haveBest=true;} };\nBut lambda overhead maybe inlined. Simpler: For each top, compute `long long cur` and update best initialized to LLONG_MIN. Include <climits>. Or initialize after first horizontal:\nAfter reading row S-1 (i==S-1), compute horizontal max and set best; for subsequent rows compare.\nPseudo:\nbool haveBest=false;\n...\nif(i>=S-1){\n  long long cur=0;\n  for(int c=0;c<S;c++) cur += col[c];\n  if(!haveBest || cur>best){best=cur; haveBest=true;}\n  for(int j=S;j<n;j++){\n    cur += col[j] - col[j-S];\n    if(cur>best) best=cur;\n  }\n}\nAt end print best. Since n>S>=1, at least one top.\n\nNeed ensure horizontal loop `for (int j = S; j < n; ++j)` because window start max n-S, last right index n-1. For each new j as right edge? Let's re-evaluate recurrence carefully:\nWe want windows starting positions t=0..n-S.\nInitial cur for t=0: sum col[0..S-1].\nFor next t=1: add col[S], remove col[0]. So loop variable `right = S; right < n; ++right`: cur += col[right] - col[right-S]; update best. This corresponds to start = right-S+1? At first iteration right=S, new window [1..S], yes start=1. Last iteration right=n-1, new window [n-S .. n-1]. Good.\nSo loop `for (int j = S; j < n; ++j) { cur += col[j] - col[j-S]; ... }`.\n\nNeed maybe unroll horizontal loop by 2/4 to improve ILP? The recurrence is serial but compiler may not vectorize due dependency. We can manually use two accumulators for even and odd windows? Let's see if beneficial.\n\nFor each top, we need max over all starts t. Recurrence cur_t = sum col[t..t+S-1]. Could compute using prefix sums to allow independent operations:\nprefix[0]=0; for j=0..n-1 prefix[j+1]=prefix[j]+col[j]; then for t=0..n-S, val=prefix[t+S]-prefix[t].\nComputing prefix is serial N adds. Then window loop has no dependency except best reduction and can be vectorized/ILP. Total 2N operations vs sliding ~2N (initial S + n-S updates = n). Actually sliding: initial sum S plus n-S iterations each one add/sub => total n operations per top? Wait initial cur requires summing S columns, then n-S updates -> total S+(n-S)=n additions/subtractions. Prefix method: prefix N adds + window loop (n-S+1) subtracts = ~2N. More ops but parallelizable. Sliding has serial chain of length n-S; for n=8000 and 4 cores? Single-thread, dependency latency maybe significant but integer add low. Could unroll sliding by processing two independent windows using direct sums every other start with block prefix? Not worth.\n\nAlternative compute horizontal max via maintaining a deque? no.\n\nCould use SIMD-friendly approach: For each top i, col array length n; we need maximum of S-length window sum. We can compute `cur` for all starts using vectorized sliding window algorithm:\n- Compute initial cur0.\n- Then for j from 1 to n-S, cur_j = cur_{j-1} + col[j+S-1] - col[j-1]. Serial dependency on cur only; but additions/subtractions can be grouped: `cur += (col[j+S-1] - col[j-1])`. The difference loads independent and could be computed ahead. Compiler may software pipeline.\nCould unroll by 4:\nwhile(j+3 < n) {\n  long long d0 = col[j+S-1]-col[j-1];\n  long long d1 = col[j+S]-col[j]; // wait for next iteration j+1: add col[(j+1)+S-1]=col[j+S], remove col[j]\n...\nBut cur updates sequential; however we can compute all differences first into local vars then apply:\nlong long c0=cur;\nc0 += d0; update best\nc0 += d1; ...\nStill serial but fewer loop overhead. Compiler likely does.\n\nCould use multiple accumulators by splitting windows? For each top, maximum of window sums could be computed using block decomposition: divide columns into blocks of size B, precompute prefix within block and suffix etc to compute max in O(n) with independent operations. Overkill.\n\nGiven prior best ~0.011 seconds for N maybe small? Baseline .133; our algorithm likely fast enough. Need not over-optimize.\n\nNeed consider OpenMP parallelization across rows? Runtime has 4 cores but problem statement says sequential implementation and speedup measured single program, could use threads? The official rules allow modify any way except stated otherwise; no restriction on OpenMP for Problem B? It doesn't mention parallel restrictions. But role A memory locality etc maybe not thread. However using OMP_NUM_THREADS=4 might help but overhead for small N and correctness with reduction. Could implement `#pragma omp parallel for` over top rows after reading all matrix, but our streaming algorithm processes sequentially; to parallelize need store full matrix or process chunks. Not necessary.\n\nNeed ensure no use of non-standard features. C++17 okay likely g++. Use `std::vector<int> buf((size_t)S * n);` If S*n maybe 0? S>0. Good.\nPotential memory allocation failure if huge; baseline also allocates N^2, so same worst-case.\n\nLet's compare with prior experiments: They had streaming ring buffer of S rows and incremental column-sum updates combined into one pass per element (81e9) valid mean .012. Our algorithm is essentially that but maybe improved horizontal max? 81e likely similar; score .917 vs best .9245 from alignment/unroll. We can incorporate cache-line alignment and unrolling to improve.\n\nRole A goal: memory locality, loop order, data layout and blocking. Candidate should focus on these. We can propose:\n- Flat ring buffer of S rows (data layout) instead of full matrix.\n- Single pass over input with column-sum incremental updates (loop fusion / memory locality).\n- Horizontal sliding window max computed per completed row using sequential col array.\n- Maybe align buffers to cache line and use `restrict`? In C++ no restrict but can use raw pointers. Could add `alignas(64)` for vectors? For vector<long long> col, alignment not guaranteed beyond default; we can allocate with `std::vector<long long> col(n);` maybe heap aligned 16 only. Use custom aligned allocator or static? n dynamic. Not necessary but could help.\n- Unroll horizontal loop by 4 to improve ILP.\n\nLet's design final code robustly and perhaps include unrolling for horizontal max:\nFor each top, after updating col:\nlong long cur=0;\nfor(int c=0;c<S;c++) cur += col[c];\nif(cur>best) best=cur;\nint j=S;\n// Unroll by 4? Need handle n-S maybe not multiple. Could do while(j+3 < n):\nwhile (j + 3 < n) {\n    long long d0 = col[j] - col[j-S];\n    cur += d0; if(cur>best) best=cur;\n    ++j;\n    long long d1 = col[j] - col[j-S]; // j now old+1, subtract col[(old+1)-S]\n...\n}\nBut careful with variable updates. Simpler:\nfor (int j=S; j<n; ++j) { cur += col[j] - col[j-S]; if(cur>best) best=cur; }\nCompiler unrolls maybe.\n\nCould use `#pragma GCC ivdep`? Not portable but g++ on ARM64 likely supports. Avoid.\n\nNeed ensure scanner's `getChar` method not too slow due to function call per char; it is inline and compiler will optimize. Could implement as macro or lambda with local buffer pointer for speed:\nint nextInt() {\n    while (pos < len && buf[pos] <= ' ') ++pos;\n    if (pos >= len) { ... refill? }\n...\n}\nBut our class uses fread per buffer fill; method call inlined.\n\nLet's write final code:\n\n#include <cstdio>\n#include <vector>\n\nclass FastScanner {\npublic:\n    static const size_t BUFSIZE = 1 << 20;\n    char buf[BUFSIZE];\n    size_t pos, len;\n    FILE *f;\n    FastScanner() : pos(0), len(0), f(stdin) {}\n    inline int getChar() {\n        if (pos >= len) {\n            len = fread(buf, 1, BUFSIZE, f);\n            pos = 0;\n            if (len == 0) return EOF;\n        }\n        return static_cast<unsigned char>(buf[pos++]);\n    }\n    inline int nextInt() {\n        int c = getChar();\n        while (c <= ' ' && c != EOF) c = getChar();\n        bool neg = false;\n        if (c == '-') { neg = true; c = getChar(); }\n        long long x = 0;\n        while (c >= '0' && c <= '9') {\n            x = x * 10 + (c - '0');\n            c = getChar();\n        }\n        return static_cast<int>(neg ? -x : x);\n    }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    FastScanner fs;\n    int n = fs.nextInt();\n    int s = fs.nextInt();\n\n    std::vector<long long> col(n, 0);\n    // ring buffer for the last S rows\n    std::vector<int> buf((size_t)s * n);\n\n    long long best = 0;\n    bool haveBest = false;\n\n    int slot = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n        size_t base = (size_t)slot * n;\n        int* row = buf.data() + base;\n        const bool removeOld = (i >= s);\n        if (removeOld) {\n            for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) col[j] -= row[j];\n        }\n        for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n            int v = fs.nextInt();\n            row[j] = v;\n            col[j] += v;\n        }\n        if (++slot == s) slot = 0;\n\n        if (i >= s - 1) {\n            long long cur = 0;\n            for (int j = 0; j < s; ++j) cur += col[j];\n            if (!haveBest || cur > best) { best = cur; haveBest = true; }\n            for (int j = s; j < n; ++j) {\n                cur += col[j] - col[j - s];\n                if (cur > best) best = cur;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    std::printf(\"%lld\\n\", best);\n    return 0;\n}\n\nNeed check when `s` maybe equals n? Constraint S<N, so no. If s=1, ring buffer size n; slot wraps every row; removeOld true for i>=1 subtract previous single row then add current -> col becomes current row. Horizontal loop j from1 to n-1: cur = col[0], update windows length 1. Good.\n\nPotential issue with `if (++slot == s) slot=0;` when s maybe large, okay.\nNeed if input has extra whitespace after last number, scanner nextInt not called beyond matrix, fine.\nNeed if N,S line read fails? no.\n\nCould improve horizontal max by using prefix sums to allow vectorization. Let's benchmark mentally: For each top i (n-S+1 ~ up to 8000), horizontal loop length n-S ~ up to 7999; total ~64M iterations with serial dependency and branch for best. Branch predictable? Values random, branch mispredict maybe but okay. Could avoid branch using `if(cur>best) best=cur` compiler may use conditional select (csel on ARM). Good.\n\nCould unroll horizontal loop by processing two windows per iteration to reduce loop overhead:\nfor (int j = s; j + 1 < n; j += 2) {\n    cur += col[j] - col[j-s];\n    if(cur>best) best=cur;\n    cur += col[j+1] - col[j+1-s];\n    if(cur>best) best=cur;\n}\nfor (; j<n; ++j) ...\nThis still serial but fewer branches/loop overhead. Could do 4x:\nint j=s;\nfor (; j + 3 < n; j += 4) {\n   cur += col[j] - col[j-s]; if(cur>best) best=cur;\n   cur += col[j+1] - col[j+1-s]; if(cur>best) best=cur;\n   cur += col[j+2] - col[j+2-s]; if(cur>best) best=cur;\n   cur += col[j+3] - col[j+3-s]; if(cur>best) best=cur;\n}\nfor (; j<n; ++j) ...\nThis may improve. But note `col[j+1-s]` etc valid since j>=s, so indices >=0. Good.\nSimilarly initial sum over s columns could be unrolled but compiler.\n\nCould also fuse removal and addition loops to one pass:\nif(removeOld){\n  for j: int old=row[j]; col[j] -= old; // then later add new\n}\nfor j read/add...\nThis is two passes per row (subtract all, then read/add). Could combine into single loop if we know old values before overwrite and need read new value:\nif(removeOld){\n  for j: int v=fs.nextInt(); col[j] += v - row[j]; row[j]=v;\n} else {\n  for j: int v=fs.nextInt(); col[j]+=v; row[j]=v;\n}\nThis avoids separate subtraction pass and improves memory locality (one loop over row). Let's implement:\nif (i >= s) {\n    for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n        int v = fs.nextInt();\n        col[j] += static_cast<long long>(v) - row[j];\n        row[j] = v;\n    }\n} else {\n    for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n        int v = fs.nextInt();\n        col[j] += v;\n        row[j] = v;\n    }\n}\nThis is better. Need ensure `col[j] += v - row[j]` with int subtraction could overflow? v and row are ints, difference within [-4e9,4e9], fits long long if cast before subtract: `col[j] += (long long)v - row[j];`. Use that.\nThis single pass per row is ideal.\n\nCould further unroll inner read loop by 4 to improve ILP:\nfor(j=0;j+3<n;j+=4){ int v0=fs.nextInt(); ... }\nBut scanner sequential and col updates independent; compiler may unroll. Not necessary but could help. However manual unrolling with function calls might be messy.\n\nNeed maybe use `std::vector<int> buf((size_t)s * n);` if s*n large, default initialization zeroes all memory (cost O(SN)). We will overwrite every slot before reading? Ring buffer slots are written as rows arrive; but vector constructor value-initializes to 0 for ints, causing extra memory writes. Could avoid by using `std::unique_ptr<int[]>` with `new int[s*n]` default-initialized (no initialization) or `malloc`. This can reduce overhead significantly for large S*N. Prior experiment 81e used \"flat uninitialized ring buffer\" to eliminate zero-init writes. Let's use raw allocation:\nint* buf = static_cast<int*>(std::malloc(sizeof(int) * (size_t)s * n));\nif (!buf) return 1;\n...\nstd::free(buf);\nNeed include <cstdlib>. This avoids initialization. Since we only read row[j] when i>=s, at that point slot contains a previously written row (because ring buffer size s and exactly s rows have been written before i=s), so safe.\nSimilarly col vector initialized to zeros needed; use `std::vector<long long> col(n, 0);` okay.\n\nCould also allocate buf with posix_memalign for cache alignment:\nvoid* mem=nullptr;\nif(posix_memalign(&mem,64,(size_t)s*n*sizeof(int)) !=0) return 1;\nint* buf=(int*)mem;\n...\nfree(mem);\nNeed include <cstdlib> and maybe <unistd.h>? posix_memalign in stdlib on Linux. ARM64 likely. But not necessary; malloc returns aligned to max_align_t (16 bytes), enough for ints. Cache-line alignment of row starts may help but rows length n*4, if n odd alignment varies. Could allocate extra padding per row to align each row? That complicates indexing and memory. Not needed.\n\nCould use `std::unique_ptr<int[]> buf(new int[(size_t)s*n]);` new int[] default-initializes? Actually `new int[N]` default-initializes, for fundamental types no initialization if no parentheses; yes leaves uninitialized. Use:\nint* buf = new int[(size_t)s * n];\n...\ndelete[] buf;\nThis is simpler and no zero-init. Include <new> not needed.\nLet's use `std::unique_ptr<int[]> buf(new int[(size_t)s*n]);` but unique_ptr constructor with raw pointer; include memory. Or manual delete.\n\nNeed ensure if allocation fails, program may crash; baseline also would throw bad_alloc maybe. Fine.\n\nPotential issue: We read all N rows and process horizontal max after each completed window. The output best is updated as we go. At end print. Good.\n\nLet's think about correctness proof:\nInvariant before reading row i (0-index):\n- `col[j]` = sum of matrix values in column j over the last min(i, s) rows? More precisely after processing previous row i-1, if i<=s then col=sum rows 0..i-1; if i>s then col=sum rows i-s..i-1.\nWhen reading row i:\n- If i>=s, ring slot contains row i-s (because each slot reused every s rows). Subtract it from col, add new row -> col = sum rows i-s+1..i. If i<s, just add -> col=sum rows 0..i.\nAfter read if i>=s-1, col is exactly S consecutive rows ending at i: rows i-S+1..i (for i=S-1 first full window). Horizontal sliding computes max over all horizontal windows of length S in these rows. Thus covers every top row t=0..n-S when i=t+S-1. Correct.\n\nNeed maybe if s*n allocation with size_t but `s` and `n` int; cast before multiply to avoid overflow: `(size_t)s * n`. Good.\nPotential issue scanner's buffer size 1<<20 as static const inside class, object on stack ~1MB okay. Could be large but fine.\n\nLet's consider if using `long long best = 0; bool haveBest=false;` and update only when i>=s-1. Since s>0,n>s so at least one iteration. Good.\nCould initialize best to first horizontal after reading row s-1, no need haveBest:\nAfter loop for each top, if !haveBest set. Fine.\n\nNeed maybe include `return 0;`.\n\nNow, should we use OpenMP? No.\n\nLet's compare with prior valid experiment 81e: It likely used similar ring buffer but perhaps had bug or slower due to horizontal max not optimized. Our candidate can be slightly better by fusing subtract/add and unrolling horizontal loop. Let's add manual unroll for inner read loops maybe:\nFor removeOld branch:\nint j=0;\nfor (; j + 3 < n; j += 4) {\n    int v0 = fs.nextInt();\n    long long c0 = col[j] - row[j] + v0; // but need update col and row\n...\n}\nThis is verbose. Compiler with -O3 likely unrolls simple loops? It may not due to function calls (nextInt inlined). We can trust.\n\nCould use `#pragma GCC unroll 4` before inner loop, but portability maybe g++ only; target uses g++. However avoid pragmas.\n\nLet's write final code with fused single-pass and horizontal unroll by 4. Need ensure no mistakes.\n\nFull code:\n\n#include <cstdio>\n#include <vector>\n\nclass FastScanner {\npublic:\n    static const size_t BUFSIZE = 1 << 20;\n    char buf[BUFSIZE];\n    size_t pos, len;\n    FILE *f;\n\n    FastScanner() : pos(0), len(0), f(stdin) {}\n\n    inline int getChar() {\n        if (pos >= len) {\n            len = fread(buf, 1, BUFSIZE, f);\n            pos = 0;\n            if (len == 0) return EOF;\n        }\n        return static_cast<unsigned char>(buf[pos++]);\n    }\n\n    inline int nextInt() {\n        int c = getChar();\n        while (c <= ' ' && c != EOF) c = getChar();\n        bool neg = false;\n        if (c == '-') { neg = true; c = getChar(); }\n        long long x = 0;\n        while (c >= '0' && c <= '9') {\n            x = x * 10 + (c - '0');\n            c = getChar();\n        }\n        return static_cast<int>(neg ? -x : x);\n    }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    FastScanner fs;\n    int n = fs.nextInt();\n    int s = fs.nextInt();\n\n    std::vector<long long> col(n, 0LL);\n    int* buf = new int[(size_t)s * (size_t)n];\n\n    long long best = 0;\n    bool haveBest = false;\n\n    int slot = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n        size_t base = (size_t)slot * (size_t)n;\n        int* row = buf + base;\n        if (i >= s) {\n            for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n                int v = fs.nextInt();\n                col[j] += (long long)v - row[j];\n                row[j] = v;\n            }\n        } else {\n            for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n                int v = fs.nextInt();\n                col[j] += v;\n                row[j] = v;\n            }\n        }\n\n        if (++slot == s) slot = 0;\n\n        if (i >= s - 1) {\n            long long cur = 0;\n            for (int j = 0; j < s; ++j) cur += col[j];\n            if (!haveBest || cur > best) { best = cur; haveBest = true; }\n\n            int j = s;\n            // Unroll the horizontal sliding update by four.\n            for (; j + 3 < n; j += 4) {\n                cur += col[j] - col[j - s];\n                if (cur > best) best = cur;\n                cur += col[j + 1] - col[j + 1 - s];\n                if (cur > best) best = cur;\n                cur += col[j + 2] - col[j + 2 - s];\n                if (cur > best) best = cur;\n                cur += col[j + 3] - col[j + 3 - s];\n                if (cur > best) best = cur;\n            }\n            for (; j < n; ++j) {\n                cur += col[j] - col[j - s];\n                if (cur > best) best = cur;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    std::printf(\"%lld\\n\", best);\n    delete[] buf;\n    return 0;\n}\n\nNeed check unroll indices:\nAt start j=s. First update uses add col[s], remove col[0] -> window [1..s]. Then j+1=s+1: add col[s+1], remove col[1] -> window [2..s+1]. Correct.\nLast iteration if j+3 < n, e.g., j=n-4: updates windows starts? For j=n-4, previous cur corresponds start = (j-s?) Let's derive recurrence invariant before each update at index `right`: cur is sum col[right-s .. right-1]? Actually after initial cur for window [0..s-1], loop variable j=s. At top of iteration with current j, we add col[j] and remove col[j-s], resulting window start = (j-s+1), end=j. So before update at j, cur corresponds previous window ending j-1: cols [(j-s) .. (j-1)]. For j=n-4, after updates for j=s..n-5? Let's see unrolled first iteration handles j=s,s+1,s+2,s+3; next j+=4. Eventually last full group maybe j=n-4,n-3,n-2,n-1 if n-s multiple? Condition `j + 3 < n` means max j = n-4, so updates right indices n-4..n-1, resulting windows ending up to n-1 start n-S. Good. If n=5,s=3: initial cur cols0-2; loop j=3 (j+3=6<5 false), tail j=3 add col3 remove col0 -> window [1..3]; j=4 add col4 remove col1 -> window [2..4]. Correct.\nNeed if s maybe n? no.\n\nPotential issue: `delete[] buf;` after printf, okay. If allocation fails new throws and program terminates; fine.\n\nCould use `std::vector<long long> col(n);` default zero-initializes to 0 because vector(size) value-inserts elements -> zeros for fundamental types. Good.\nNeed include <cstdio>, <vector>. No algorithm needed.\n\nLet's think about scanner: It reads first int n, then s; if there is leading whitespace, nextInt handles. After reading last matrix number, it may have consumed one char beyond (the delimiter) but no issue. `getChar` when buffer exhausted refills. Good.\nPotential bug in `nextInt`: after parsing digits, it stops at non-digit and leaves that character unread; next call's getChar will read same whitespace/delimiter and skip via while <= ' '. Fine.\n\nCould there be plus sign? Input integers no plus likely. If plus, scanner would treat as c='+' not digit/whitespace, x=0 then return 0 incorrectly. Problem says integers with whitespace; could include negative but not plus. Official examples positive only. To be robust, handle